        <div class="container-body   pb-2 pr-3 pt-2 pl-1 c-Container--guttered">
            
                
                                    
                                    
        
            
                    

    <div id="container-91573-item-91589" class="container-item">
        
                        
        
        
        
        
		
        
            
    	
	        

	
    
	    
	        <div style="" class="" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		
	            
                                    
                                    
        
            
                    
        
            
                    
            

    

    

	
		 	<div class="component component-content-item component-content-summary abstract_or_excerpt">
		        
    	
                

    <div class="content-box box no-border  no-header vertical-margin-bottom null">
        
        <div class="content-box-body ">
            
                    
            
        
                
			
				
					<div sendCounterEvent="true" data-datatype="abstract" data-docuri="/book/9781035323036/chapter7.xml" class="counterData"></div>
				
			
           <section class="abstract"><p>This chapter interrogates the question of what defines predictive policing. Our point of departure is notions in Science and Technology Studies that attend to how boundary objects such as predictive policing are produced through the interrelation of politics, sociotechnical imaginaries, and contestations. Through ethnographic inquiry on the empirical case of the POL-INTEL platform of the Danish police, we follow the evolution and demarcations in the (re)-classifications of POL-INTEL from predictive to non-predictive. In so doing, we situate POL-INTEL in the wider international context regarding the development of predictive policing and investigate the boundary work between predictive policing and similar concepts such as Intelligence-Led Policing. Concisely, we argue that the development of the notion of predictive policing is fundamentally unstable and tied to political and economic interests.</p></section>
        
            
    
        
    
    
    
                
        </div>
    </div>


            
    
		    </div>
		 

    

    

    
	


        
                
        
        
        
        
		
        
    
                

        


    
                                    
                                
	        
	        	
	        </div>
	    
	    
	    
	    
	    
	    
	    

		
    


	     
    
        
    
                    
    </div>

    
    


                

        


    
                                    
                                
                                    
                                    
        
            
                    

    <div id="container-91573-item-91577" class="container-item">
        
                        
        
        
        
        
		
        
            
    	
	        

	
    
	    
	        <div style="" class="" role="" id="">
	        	
	        		
	            
                                    
                                    
        
            
                    
        
            
                    
            

    

    

	
		 	<div class="component component-content-item component-content-html ">
		        
    	
                

    <div class="content-box box no-border  no-header vertical-margin-bottom null">
        
        <div class="content-box-body ">
            
                    
        
		<div data-datatype="fthtml" data-docuri="/book/9781035323036/chapter7.xml" class="counterData"></div>
		<div class="bodyCssRoot" id="contentRoot"><div id="chapterBody">
<div class="section" id="sec8_1">
<h2 id="d76136626e13146">Introduction</h2>
<div class="p">When Palantir Technologies won a public tender in 2016 to customize their Gotham platform for the Danish police, dubbed POL-INTEL, a public debate erupted in Denmark over the notion of predictive policing. Critical voices raised warnings about predictive policing as a mass surveillance tool (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000049" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000049">Kulager, 2021</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000055" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000055">Lehmann Hylleberg, 2021</a>). According to the police narrative, the procurement of the new digital police platform would provide the police with a “super weapon” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000048" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000048">Kulager, 2017</a>) in the war against crime. POL-INTEL is also related to the structural reforms that took place in the Danish police during the last 20 years and have led to a paradigm shift toward a so-called Intelligence-Led Policing (ILP) (Volquartzen, 2018). ILP refers to a variety of strategies aimed at proactive rather than reactive policing (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000032" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000032">Fyfe et al., 2018</a>). A unifying definition of the concept reads as:<blockquote>
<p>a business model and managerial philosophy where data analysis and crime intelligence are pivotal to an objective, decision-making framework that facilitates crime and problem reduction, disruption and prevention through both strategic management and effective enforcement strategies that target prolific and serious offenders. (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000064" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000064">Ratcliffe, 2016</a>, p. 6)</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<p>In the Danish context, ILP is something that the police take pride in and market themselves as international pioneers of (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000081" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000081">Sausdal, 2022</a>). Predictability is an inherent attribute of ILP, which relies on intelligence analysis of past criminal offenses as well as heuristic interpretation of the criminal environment, assessment of risks for crime repetition, human intuition, and speculation on future scenarios (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000032" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000032">Fyfe et al., 2018</a>). In this sense, the question of what demarcates (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000035" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000035">Gieryn, 1983</a>) ILP from predictive policing is of central concern for the POL-INTEL case.</p>
<p>In this chapter, we situate critiques of predictive policing in the Danish context and investigate the rise and fall of the notion of predictive policing through <span id="page_131" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">131</span></span></span>the case of the POL-INTEL platform by asking the question: <i>what is predictive policing?</i> This question is inspired by the age-old question in epistemology: “what is true knowledge?” which opens a field of inquiry into different ways of knowing and producing knowledge. Being skeptical of any kind of positivist approach to knowledge production, the chapter does not search for a true definition of predictive policing. Rather, as we will show, what defines predictive policing is diachronically juxtaposed to a variety of actors that interpret and demarcate a set of policing concepts in the digital era. These concepts interplay with politics, imaginaries, and trials of power, linking policing with digital innovations in automation. In this context, we do not merely aim to reassemble (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000054" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000054">Latour, 2007</a>) definitions of predictive policing but also to explore the relation between policing, society, crime, the state, and the role of private enterprises such as Palantir. Aligned with that, this chapter aims to position POL-INTEL in the universe of concepts and theories that investigate predictive policing in contemporary critical academic literature.</p>
<div class="section" id="sec8_2">
<h3 id="d76136626e13197">Predictive Policing: <i>Precrime</i> in Action or…</h3>
<p>Already in the 17th<sup></sup>century, several calculative instruments, including forecasts and statistical estimates, were born to measure in statistical terms phenomena such as the birth and mortality rate, risk, deviancy, and crime. These instruments, which <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000031" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000031">Foucault (2003)</a> identified as <i>technologies of power</i> or <i>biopolitics</i>, were adopted by the Western sovereign state during the 17th–18th<sup></sup>centuries for managing humans in large groups and transforming them into a <i>population</i>. Key to this adaptation process was the use of scientific methods, which made the population predictable and manageable. Crime prevention and law enforcement were at the heart of this process. Dubber &amp; Valverde (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000025" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000025">2006</a>, p. 4) argue that the “police works as a sort of temporal hinge-word, allowing the governance of the past to be articulated with the governance of the future.” The scientification of crime management had a particular role to play in attempting to predict future criminal events (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000031" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000031">Foucault, 2003</a>). This is most famously captured in Cesare Beccaria's work from 1764, where he conceptualized a criminological model based on rationality rather than religious superstition.</p>
<p>From Beccaria to Guerry and Quételet, founders of moral statistics, correlations of crimes with different social variables in the first third of the 19th century signified the birth of scientific criminology. This new biopolitical technique understood crime as subject to scientific analysis and verification through statistical inquiry (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000102" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000102">Završnik &amp; Klobasa, 2021</a>). Criminology has since this early period been characterized by a complex history that evolved into a variety of political and epistemic positions, not to mention differing methodological strategies. One could argue that criminology developed in many directions throughout the 20th century, from critical (e.g., <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000021" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000021">Cohen, 1998</a>) to <span id="page_132" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">132</span></span></span>administrative criminology in support of the state (e.g., <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000056" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000056">Mayhew, 2016</a>), to proactive policing (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000107" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000107">Weisburd et al., 2019</a>). A full account of these lies outside the scope of this chapter. However, a red thread can be traced from the genesis of the scientification of criminology to theories such as “criminologies of place” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000037" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000037">Haggerty</a>, 2004, p. 218) that provided “the analytic framework for a series of situationally specific strategies designed to enhance security” (ibid., p. 218). Interlaced with criminology of place is the so-called crime science movement (see, for example, <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000083" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000083">Sherman, 1998</a>, <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000084" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000084">2002</a>) that argued for stronger criminological scientific methods to enhance policymaking and evidence-based methodologies. What is relevant for this chapter are the transformations that criminological techniques and technologies underwent to draw level with changes in the environment of crime. These transformations refer to the ways that would make criminology more directly relevant to crime control and prevention (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000020" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000020">Clarke, 2004</a>).</p>
<p>Such criminological transformations have been the catalysts for producing the quantitative models that were subsequently digitalized and automated in the modern era (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000026" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000026">Egbert &amp; Leese, 2021</a>). For instance, <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000094" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000094">Van Brakel (2016)</a> explains that the mainstreaming of situational crime prevention theories during the 1990s led to the advancement of hot spot analysis and later to the development of one of the first “predictive policing applications” in the United Kingdom, the so-called <i>ProMap</i>. Such advances would most famously coalesce around the concept of <i>predictive policing</i>. Following the global financial crash in 2008, predictive policing took over police departments in the US and rapidly attracted international attention. Since then, law enforcement authorities have deployed it globally, also sparking serious criticism concerning issues of racialized and militarized policing (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000099" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000099">Wilson, 2019</a>), human bias in the algorithm (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000017" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000017">Caliskan-Islam et al., 2017</a>), and threats to fundamental civil liberties (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000101" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000101">Završnik, 2017</a>).</p>
<p>The notion of predictive policing began in earnest in the US with Los Angeles Police Department (LAPD) Chief William Bratton, who, together with Sean Malinowski, popularized the term in an article entitled “Police Performance Management in Practice: Taking COMPSTAT to the Next Level” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000012" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000012">Bratton &amp; Malinowski, 2008</a>)<i>.</i> One of their main arguments was that big data analytics would bring about a revolution in police practice, moving “from near real-time analysis to true real-time analysis” (ibid., p. 264). In this way, according to the same publication, police work would be able to perform more accurate and reliable probability modeling and hence produce fewer victims, less crime, and better police leadership. This early but symptomatic account of predictive policing activated a thread of “techno-solutionism” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000059" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000059">Morozov, 2013</a>) that extends into the present (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000099" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000099">Wilson, 2019</a>), that is, with the right technologies, every crime appears to be predictable. Bratton and Malinowski's article was also foundational in importing sociotechnical imaginaries (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000043" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000043">Jasanoff, 2015a</a>) <span id="page_133" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">133</span></span></span>of the power of big data from the world of business into the field of policing (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000099" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000099">Wilson, 2019</a>). The most pronounced versions of these utopian imaginaries suggest that the power of data analytics and predictive policing will create a crime-free world (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000026" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000026">Egbert &amp; Leese, 2021</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000033" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000033">Flyverbom &amp; Hansen, 2019</a>). Thus, expectations and dreams of a particular social order to be achieved in and by technoscientific means have been projected onto predictive policing.</p>
<p>As the notion of predictive policing began to gain momentum, an inflation of definitions cropped up, including the RAND Corporation's influential definition: “predictive policing is the application of analytical techniques—particularly quantitative techniques—to identify likely targets for police intervention and prevent crime or solve past crimes by making statistical predictions” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000062" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000062">Perry et al., 2013</a>, p. 1). Perry et al. also account for different types of predictive analytics, including hot spots, and claim that predictive policing is defined by data sets so large that no single analyst could “recall them without the assistance of a computer program” (ibid., p. 14). Others pose alternative definitions, including: “the term predictive policing … refers to the application of quantitative techniques to foretell where crimes will take place in the short-term future” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000018" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000018">Camacho-Collados &amp; Liberatore, 2015</a>) or “given historical crime incident data for a collection of regions, decide how to allocate patrol officers to areas to detect crime” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000027" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000027">Ensign et al., 2018</a>, p. 2).</p>
<p>In line with Meijer &amp; Wessels (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000057" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000057">2019</a>), we argue that there is no unanimous definition of the concept of predictive policing. There has also been no general debate around definitions that explain what clearly constitutes one tool or another as <i>predictive</i>. Some scholars have instead attempted to avoid stating specific, all-encompassing definitions. For instance, <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000046" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000046">Kaufmann et al. (2019)</a> note a variety of different styles of predictive policing, which rely on different criminological axioms, different data, and different focus subjects. Inspired by <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000052" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000052">Latour (1987)</a>, we approach prediction in action through ethnographic inquiry rather than abstractly contributing a new definition.</p>
</div>
<div class="section" id="sec8_3">
<h3 id="d76136626e13346">A Brief History of Predictive Policing in Action</h3>
<p>
<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000051" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000051">Latour (1983)</a> approaches science as politics by other means. This can also be traced in the attempts to scientize crime control through predictive policing (e.g., <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000098" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000098">Weisburd, 2011</a>). This scientification process (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000028" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000028">Ericson &amp; Shearing, 1986</a>) also implicates specific police and private capital interrelations as a plethora of different companies produce and promote predictive systems (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000063" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000063">Pinard et al., 2021</a>). For us, the involvement of police officers with Big Tech and academia, promoting specific policing and criminological tactics, is far from agnostic and apolitical.</p>
<p>The scientification of policing through the deployment of predictive policing platforms also involves political choices and important consequences. <span id="page_134" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">134</span></span></span>Foremost among them is the emergence of feedback loops where biased police action feeds predictive algorithms that suggest further police intervention on vulnerable groups and communities (e.g., <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000029" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000029">Ferguson, 2017</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000027" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000027">Ensign et al., 2018</a>). Criticisms of feedback loops were deployed as a main argument in the 2018 ban on predictive policing in Los Angeles (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000015" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000015">Brayne, 2021</a>). However, this ban did not signify the end of predictive analytics in Californian policing. After the ban, LAPD Chief Bratton launched a new concept—Precision Policing (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000013" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000013">Bratton &amp; Anderson, 2018</a>)—which “consists of two important components: an intelligence-led investigative component and a neighborhood coordination component. The intelligence-led investigative side of this policy uses predictive analysis of all crimes” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000004" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000004">Ashraf, 2020</a>, p. xv). Similarly, a broad wave of concept production has proliferated in recent times. These different concepts, such as Smart Policing (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000022" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000022">Coldren et al., 2013</a>) and Risk Terrain Modeling (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000016" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000016">Calhoun, 2023</a>), typically draw upon a range of predictive analytics and techniques whose differentiation from predictive policing is difficult to disentangle.</p>
<p>In the same context, the major predictive policing company PredPol was renamed Geolitica, and in a press release, the company stated that “[the term “prediction”] has broadened to include activities—such as facial recognition or ‘predicting’ that certain individuals will commit crimes—with which we are not aligned; even the use of the word ‘predictive’ itself does not accurately describe our business” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000034" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000034">Geolitica, 2021</a>). Yet, Geolitica, in the very next sentence of the same press release, explained that rather than performing predictions, they “were conducting risk assessments, using what-where-when historical crime data to identify the highest-risk locations of specified crimes by time of day and day of week” (ibid.). In this sense, Geolitica demarcates individual-based predictive policing from place-based risk assessments. However, Geolitica was always engaged in and marketed itself as a provider of place-based predictions. What differentiated this latter approach and their earlier championing of predictive policing remains ambiguous. The cases of PredPol and Geolitica, as well as the introduction of alternative concepts such as Precision Policing, Smart Policing, and risk terrain modeling, are indicative of a wider international redefinition, revision, and reconceptualization of predictive policing.</p>
<p>Moving along, the issue of how predictive policing is demarcated (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000035" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000035">Gieryn, 1983</a>) from other forms of computer-assisted and analysis-based policing techniques is specifically related in this chapter to ILP. ILP is the preferred term for the strategic doctrine of the Danish police (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000075" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000075">Rigspolitiet, 2018</a>). ILP co-exists and is connected to predictive policing (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000064" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000064">Ratcliffe, 2016</a>). Ratcliffe, one of the main proponents of ILP, argues that predictive policing is vitally close to ILP: “in many cases, predictive policing grids (as determined by the software) become the target of traditional focused patrol tactics: another example of technology enhancing traditional strategies rather than driving new <span id="page_135" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">135</span></span></span>approaches” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000064" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000064">Ratcliffe, 2016</a>, p. 147). Ratcliffe himself employed predictive policing in practice to improve patrol efficiency in Philadelphia (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000065" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000065">Ratcliffe et al., 2021</a>). Similarly, <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000079" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000079">Rummens et al. (2017)</a> claim that “as a policy strategy, predictive policing can be situated within the scope of intelligence-led policing (ILP)” (p. 1).</p>
<p>Predictive analytics are present in ILP as much as in the variety of other post-predictive policing concepts we have described. All of these tend to combine several components utilized by predictive policing, such as hot spot modeling, quantitative methods, data and intelligence analysis, and focusing on targeting prolific offenders and risk areas. Therefore, we claim that the question of what predictive policing is—or is not—is related to <i>boundary work</i>
<a id="ref_ch7-fn1" href="#ch7-fn1">
<sup>1</sup>
</a> (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000035" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000035">Gieryn, 1983</a>) through a complex matrix of political contestations, the economic interests of companies such as Geolitica, and scientific interpretative flexibility.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="section" id="sec8_4">
<h2 id="d76136626e13439">A boundary imaginary? A conceptual framework</h2>
<p>To assist our discussion of what constitutes predictive policing and to reassemble the relationship between technology, law enforcement, and state-making, we will employ a few concepts inspired by Science and Technology Studies (STS). Our conceptual aim is to account for how technologies are political and social (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000100" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000100">Winner, 1980</a>) and contingent rather than universal (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000052" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000052">Latour, 1987</a>). In this sense, STS approaches underline the close relationship between philosophical arguments and politics (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000082" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000082">Shapin &amp; Schaffer, 2011</a>), as well as how private and political factors partake in the constitution of sociotechnical systems (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000042" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000042">Hughes, 1987</a>). Such conceptualizations have also been deployed in the study of predictive policing to investigate predictive systems as political technologies that perform specific sociotechnical effects (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000026" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000026">Egbert &amp; Leese, 2021</a>) in and through a wider police assemblage (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000047" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000047">Kaufmann &amp; Leese, 2021</a>). This differs from theories that take law enforcement and its associated technologies for granted as an unproblematic essential in creating efficient security, which has been a prevalent perspective in Computer Science and contemporary forms of place-based, quantitative criminological work that aim to improve police efficiency through building predictive models (e.g., <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000058" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000058">Mohler, 2014</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000077" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000077">Rosser et al., 2017</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000036" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000036">Goodson &amp; Hoyer-Leitzel, 2021</a>). We argue that the use and adoption of such technologies are the result of a complex configuration process that <span id="page_136" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">136</span></span></span>mobilizes economic and material investments, legal arrangements, privacy rights, and sociocultural imaginaries (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000067" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000067">Redden, 2018</a>).</p>
<p>In particular, the work of <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000043" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000043">Jasanoff (2015a)</a> on sociotechnical imaginaries has become an important tool in unraveling the interrelation between technology and society (e.g., <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000011" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000011">Bowman, 2015</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000040" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000040">Hockenhull &amp; Cohn, 2021</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000078" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000078">Rudek, 2022</a>). The adoption of digital tools in law enforcement implies that knowledge production can be visualized, understood, and accounted for in several new ways and through multiple imaginaries that co-exist in tension or in a dialectical relationship (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000043" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000043">Jasanoff, 2015a</a>). In this chapter, we critically account for and conceptually problematize the diversity in sociotechnical imaginaries related to predictive analytics inscribed in the POL-INTEL platform and generated, institutionally stabilized, and performed by shared understandings of forms of crime and social order (ibid.). We investigate the epistemological shifts in the concept of policing prompted by sociotechnical imaginaries related to predictive analytics.</p>
<p>In particular, it is vital to attend to how sociotechnical imaginaries are rarely unitary but typically split between a utopian and dystopian version (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000086" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000086">Sovacool &amp; Hess, 2017</a>). Seen through this lens, predictive policing is imagined both through the promise of a crime-free world on the one hand and the threat of Big Brother surveillance on the other (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000033" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000033">Flyverbom &amp; Hansen, 2019</a>). It is in this vein that Egbert &amp; Leese (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000026" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000026">2021</a>) acknowledge that the very concept of predictive policing has been challenging to approach due to science fiction and dystopian imaginaries inherent in the understanding of these systems. We treat predictive policing as an object that is both plastic enough to adapt to local uses, translations, and the constraints of the several law enforcement actors applying it, yet robust enough to maintain a common identity and concept across sites, police departments, and political interpretations (cf. <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000087" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000087">Star &amp; Griesemer, 1989</a>). As <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000035" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000035">Gieryn (1983)</a> suggests, rather than being objective characteristics representing an underlying real world, scientific boundaries are demarcated through the interplay of social conflict and scientific definitions.</p>
</div>
<div class="section" id="sec8_5">
<h2 id="d76136626e13516">Methodological considerations</h2>
<p>As our goal is to investigate the plasticity of the notion of predictive policing, we employ a qualitative methodological approach to relativize the specificities of the concept both globally by comparison with previous research and within our empirical case study. In so doing, we make use of interpretative research and analysis that explore the architecture of “structure–agency relationships through inquiries into meaning making” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000043" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000043">Jasanoff, 2015a</a>, p. 24). We base our study on ethnographic methods to monitor and discuss complexities in the making of the concept of predictive policing in the context of the POL-INTEL platform.<span id="page_137" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">137</span></span></span>
</p>
<p>Specifically, we have interviewed the former head of the police division of the Ministry of Justice, the former Data Protection Officer (DPO) of POL-INTEL, the POL-INTEL project owner, a former police director, the chief of a dispatch center, and an intelligence analyst in a police district, as well as an analyst located centrally in the Danish National Police. From Palantir, we have interviewed two members of the Privacy and Civil Liberties Team (PCL), as well as the head of Palantir Scandinavia. In relation to media materials, our reading has encompassed the entirety of newspaper material (both online and in print) on POL-INTEL in the Danish press, alongside a close reading of selected articles in the international press with a wide distribution (e.g., <i>New York Times</i>,<i> the Guardian</i>, etc.). In terms of document analysis, we have collected documents that are both openly available, such as transcripts of parliamentary debates related to POL-INTEL, as well as retrieved documents such as the Intelligence and Analysis Doctrine for the Danish police through Freedom of Information Act requests. This material was coded in an iterative, qualitative manner by close reading of the material that developed an initial coding, which was subsequently revised after critical reflection. Data was collected between March 2021 and October 2022.</p>
</div>
<div class="section" id="sec8_6">
<h2 id="d76136626e13538">POL-INTEL: a predictive policing platform?</h2>
<p>In the introduction of the chapter, we accounted for the development of predictive policing as a concept in an international context. In the empirical section of this chapter, we will delve deeper into the Danish case of POL-INTEL. Our point of departure is the Danish National Police's important turn toward ILP in 2013, with the establishment of intelligence units in each of the police districts (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000072" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000072">Rigspolitiet, 2013</a>). While ILP can support “reactive” investigations, its core mission statement internationally (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000064" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000064">Ratcliffe, 2016</a>) and in its Danish iteration (Interview, POL-INTEL Project Owner, September 2021) is to develop analyses to produce more proactive policing. This was the result of a long transformation period within the Danish police (starting with the 2007 Police Reform) that brought several structural changes combined with a shift to New Public Management (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000023" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000023">Degnegaard, 2010</a>). The new game in town was “efficiency, fast response time, uniform organization across districts and management by results” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000041" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000041">Holmberg, 2019</a>, p. 209). According to a Senior Intelligence Analyst, this turn to ILP came “because [of] downsizing all the time as the Danish police hoped to ‘do more with less’” (Interview, September 2022). The Danish police have related the development of ILP with the need for the police to procure new digital platforms (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000075" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000075">Rigspolitiet, 2018</a>).</p>
<p>In the words of the former DPO of the Danish police, “the credo [of POL-INTEL] was that the police should know what the police knows” (Interview, Former DPO, March 2021). That is, the police had the necessary knowledge <span id="page_138" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">138</span></span></span>but lacked the technical potential to unlock it. This is what POL-INTEL would solve. The quote articulates a concrete political problem related to achieving what is conventionally perceived as better policing, namely, the quality, visibility, and interoperability of data and the lack of capacity for doing this in the pre-POL-INTEL era. <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000042" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000042">Hughes (1987)</a> explains that problem formulations, such as the above, are not neutral descriptions. Instead, problems are connected to what range of solutions in sociotechnical systems can be anticipated. Problem formulations are related to which solutions can be <i>imagined</i> (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000043" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000043">Jasanoff, 2015a</a>).</p>
<p>This way of imagining the solutions to the problems of the Danish police precluded other alternative concepts of policing, such as the emphasis on street-level patrols and community policing, which dominated the earlier paradigm and public debate on policing in Denmark (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000005" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000005">Balvig, 2015</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000104" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000104">Volquartzen, 2013</a>). Likewise, this imaginary excludes non-police solutions to crime, such as welfare initiatives or other social solutions.</p>
<p>The transmission of the imaginary into the Danish context began with Palantir's Gotham system being marketed to a vanguard of police officials who were shown a demo of the Palantir Gotham system in Monte Carlo in 2014 (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000049" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000049">Kulager, 2021</a>). This group developed what <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000044" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000044">Jasanoff (2015b)</a>, quoting Hilgartner (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000039" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000039">2015</a>), refers to as a <i>vanguard vision</i>, a proto-imaginary that the vanguard then works to materialize. Subsequently, Danish police officials also made a field trip to the United States to visit two developers of predictive policing platforms. These were IBM in Boston and Palantir Technologies in Los Angeles. An internal police report stated that the IBM platform was inadequate for the purposes of the Danish police. Palantir Gotham, on the other hand, was deemed suitable for the needs of the police in Denmark (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000073" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000073">Rigspolitiet, 2014</a>).</p>
<p>However, there were further complications ahead before the vanguard vision could become a materialized product. In our interpretation, this is due to how the Danish police are governed, that is, the Danish police are organized at the state level. This is a vital organizational difference from, for example, the US and German paradigms and the purchases of Palantir Gotham in the US and German contexts, which are described by <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000015" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000015">Brayne (2021)</a> and Egbert and Leese (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000026" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000026">2021</a>) respectively. This means that the allocation of funds and legal status became a concern ultimately for the Danish Parliament, which approves the state budgets, including procuring technical systems and funding for the police. Initially, the Danish government was negative about a new digital platform for the police. The reason why the Danish state was unwilling to fund a new platform was due to the failure of the police in developing, in-house, a new version of the POLSAS police case handling system that serves as the basic police operations system (Interview, Senior Police Intelligence Analyst in District, September 2022). The new system, POLSAG, was “distinctively different from POLSAS in two areas: case management would be digitally based, and the system would be implemented nationwide and be accessible <span id="page_139" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">139</span></span></span>across the police districts” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000071" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000071">Rigsrevisionen, 2013</a>, p. 2). POLSAG was finally shut down in 2012 after being in development since 2007 and costing circa 567 million Danish Crowns (ibid.).</p>
<div class="p">The momentary shackling to proceed with the procurement for the Gotham platform, or any other policing system, changed after the so-called 2015 Copenhagen terrorist attack that left three people dead (including the perpetrator) and several police officers wounded. Scholars have long remarked that security events like terrorist attacks are liable to cause politicians and states to act forcefully through the implementation of new security technologies (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000080" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000080">Sausdal, 2021</a>; see also <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000002" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000002">Amoore, 2013</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000001" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000001">Agamben, 2005</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000024" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000024">Duarte, 2021</a>). In the case of Denmark, the shootings mobilized the Danish government to issue the report <i>A Strong Defense Against Terror: 12 New Initiatives Against Terror</i> that recommended the purchase of an intelligence platform for the police and the Danish secret services. In connection with the report, the government allocated 150 million DKK for the procurement of a platform (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000066" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000066">Regeringen, 2015</a>). The report explained that such a platform anticipated “…a substantial strengthening of the possibilities to identify or localize potential perpetrators before a terrorist attack or other very serious crimes are committed” (ibid., our translation). Subsequently, a tender was issued that specified:<blockquote>
<p>The platform should be able to utilize a variety of common algorithms and technologies for visualization and pattern recognition in large volumes of data—both proactively and based on user actions. This could, for example, involve temporal segmentation of data, geographical visualization and processing of data, hotspot analysis, Social Network Analysis tools, machine learning, and deep learning technologies. (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000105" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000105">Rigspolitiet (2015), our translation)</a>
</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div class="p">The predictive components specified in the quote above adhered to several of the predictive policing definitions accounted for in the introduction of this chapter, such as that of the RAND Corporation's (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000062" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000062">Perry et al., 2013</a>). Palantir Technologies finally won the tender bid as “once the actual competition started, we participated in it and you know by the end of it actually all of our competitors have dropped out because there was no way to provide this capability at this price point” (Interview, Palantir Scandinavia Head, September 2021). The contract was subsequently signed in 2016. By this point, the critique against predictive policing had not entered the Danish public debate. This is evident in the reply of the Danish police to a parliamentary query that asks if POL-INTEL will support the use of predictive policing:<blockquote>
<p>There is no question that “predictive policing” can replace or render obsolete police experience, but it is a working method that can help ensure that police patrols, etc., are used most effectively to prevent or address, for example, personal crime. The experiences from countries that have implemented “predictive policing” in their <span id="page_140" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">140</span></span></span>police forces seem to show that this working method can yield good results, including in the field of burglary crime. (Retsudvalget 2016–17 REU Alm.del Offentligt <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000068" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000068">2016</a>, our translation)</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<p>At this point, the Danish police were still comfortable using the concept of predictive policing. A representative for the police publicly envisioned through the Danish police Union's newspaper that “in the long run, the system [POL-INTEL] will also introduce completely new workflows in the Danish police and support predictive policing” (our translation, <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000010" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000010">Bjørnholdt, 2016</a>). Alongside the championing of prediction, POL-INTEL was accompanied by a utopian imaginary where the program was referred to as a “super weapon” (our translation, <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000048" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000048">Kulager, 2017</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000092" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000092">Toft, 2018</a>) in the war against crime.</p>
<div class="section" id="sec8_7">
<h3 id="d76136626e13676">Turning POL-INTEL Non-Predictive</h3>
<p>“We entered the room. Several people waited for us, both male and female police officers. One was smiling. The rest were just sitting there with an undefinable crack on their lips. ‘We want to talk about predictive policing,’ we said. This immediately electrified the atmosphere. ‘There is no such thing as predictive policing related to POL-INTEL,’ said the smiling man. Another police officer grimaced. That set the tone. If we actually wanted to go on with this fieldwork, we had to re-invent our main concept, forgetting about prediction, anticipation, and prognosis. The smiling police officer declared, ‘Intelligence-led policing, this is what we do.’ A general feeling of relief dominated the room. ‘We can live with intelligence-led policing,’ we said to ourselves” (notes from our fieldwork, first meeting with the POL-INTEL group, December 2019).</p>
<div class="p">The critique against predictive policing in general and toward Palantir specifically intensified in the United States after 2016 (e.g. <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000038" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000038">Hardy, 2016</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000090" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000090">Thomas, 2016</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000050" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000050">Lapowsky, 2018</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000076" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000076">Robitzski, 2018</a>). Imaginaries are liable to move from the national context they were developed in across borders (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000044" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000044">Jasanoff, 2015b</a>). This is true for the case of POL-INTEL as well, both regarding the original imaginaries of the platform constituting an efficient crime-fighting tool but also regarding the later dystopian imaginaries. These imaginaries emphasized the risk of mass surveillance, shadowy relations between the state and Big Tech, and bias in the algorithms. While in 2016 there were only a few solitary references to these dystopian imaginaries (e.g. <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000060" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000060">Mølsted, 2016</a>), from 2017 and onward these ideas became more common in the Danish press (e.g. Sommerand, <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000085" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000085">2017</a>; Vinther-Nielsen, <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000095" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000095">2017</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000091" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000091">Thøgersen, 2017</a>). One example of the influence of American dystopian accounts of Palantir transplanted to Denmark can be found in the major daily <i>Berlingske</i>:<span id="page_141" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">141</span></span></span>
<blockquote>
<p>The system that the Danish Police and [Secret Services] are about to set up is delivered by Palantir Technologies which have specialized in data analysis for intelligence agencies around the world. Palantir, which is founded with economic support from the American intelligence agency the CIA, has earlier been in focus as the firm has assisted the American intelligence agency the NSA with enacting [<i>opsætte</i>] massive surveillance, which top secret papers made public by the ex-intelligence analyst Edward Snowden have revealed since June 2013. (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000009" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000009">Bjørnager &amp; Breinstrupog, 2017</a>)</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<p>These articles are not merely an exercise in enlightened reflection on what predictive policing is or what POL-INTEL can or cannot accomplish. Instead, these debates threatened to imperil the process of purchasing and legalizing the platform. This is because, in 2017, the Danish Parliament was set to vote on changing the police law to legalize the use of “cross-cutting” (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000030" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000030">Folketinget, 2017</a>, our translation) data collection methods for law enforcement. This was essentially a vote on legalizing POL-INTEL. Without such a legal reform, the police and the Ministry of Justice, which governs the police, were concerned about potential legal challenges to the system (Interview, Former Ministry of Justice Police Division Head, February 2022). Critiques of POL-INTEL in the press thus threatened to convince parliamentarians to vote against its legalization.</p>
<div class="p">From 2018 and onwards, the Danish police also moved away from their earlier standpoint that POL-INTEL is a predictive policing tool. For instance, the former Data Protection Officer (DPO) explained that:<blockquote>
<p>So, there is a lot of stuff out there, about Palantir, and predictive policing, and algorithms and it's all wrong. … You need a lot of data to do prediction, so maybe you can do this in something like in the traffic, [where you] may have enough data. Predicting homicides in Denmark is completely impossible, we have like 50 a year, there is no way that you have anything that would allow you to identify where a murder may be committed, or even a burglary. (Interview, Former Data Protection Officer, March 2021)</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div class="p">In this quote, the former DPO demarcates (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000035" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000035">Gieryn, 1983</a>) what predictive policing is through the necessity of obtaining large amounts of data. His statement relies on the hypothesis that there is a true predictive policing out there, but it is one which the Danish police cannot perform, due to their lack of data on serious crimes such as murder. Similarly, in 2020, a parliamentarian for the left-wing Red-Green Alliance (<i>Enhedslisten</i>) asked the Minister of Justice if “… the Danish police's IT system provided by Palantir (POL-INTEL and PET-INTEL) uses algorithms for predictive policing, and whether it complies with UN and the Data Protection Agency's requirements for responsibility for the rights of individuals” (Retsudvalget 2020–21 REU Alm.del, <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000069" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000069">2021</a>, our <span id="page_142" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">142</span></span></span>translation). In turn, the Minister referred the question to the Danish National Police who replied that:<blockquote>
<p>The initial observation notes that the National Police have understood the question as seeking to uncover whether POL-INTEL is used to conduct forecasts regarding which individuals are expected to commit crimes and whether, if so, this is done with due consideration for individuals’ fundamental rights. In this context, the National Police can state that POL-INTEL is not used for profiling or predicting individual-level criminality, and the analysis platform is not used to predict crime patterns at an aggregated level. (our translation, ibid.)</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div class="p">The police thus reformulated the query by stating that the parliamentarian is asking whether the police perform forecasts on an individual's propensity for future crimes. With such a definition in mind, the police could reply that they were not engaging in predictive policing. The police furthermore added that they did not make crime predictions on an aggregated level. However, the police went on to state that:<blockquote>
<p>The National Police, however, generally works toward the police force operating in a more knowledge-based and data-driven manner. When the police conduct analyses in this context, they work based on hypotheses derived from existing data. For instance, if patrolling needs to be planned to target a higher number of burglaries in a specific area, this planning may be based on existing data regarding where and when burglary crimes typically occur in a particular geographic area. (our translation, ibid.)</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div class="p">The police thus stated that they <i>did</i> utilize data on past crimes, combining it with hypotheses to make analyses for planning police work. The use of historical crime data to plan patrols is one of the predictive techniques most commonly featured in predictive policing (e.g., Perry, <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000062" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000062">2013</a>; <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000026" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000026">Egbert &amp; Leese, 2021</a>). In that sense, the police distinguished what predictive policing is and is not, and thus carefully navigated the question the Minister had referred to them. When confronted with this contradiction in terms, some members of the police and Palantir developed an alternative, new boundary for what constitutes prediction. They did so by suggesting that calling POL-INTEL a predictive policing tool “emaciates the concept” (Interview, Palantir Civil Liberties Representative #1, November 2022), as:<blockquote>
<p>… companies like PredPol, … are trying to make an argument that you can use statistical techniques, regressions or whatever to build models that project what will happen in a point in space and time in the future. As opposed to drawing broad inferences that may be part of a broader context of considerations that figure into how many a police agency hires or how many patrol cars you have or other general logistics questions which I view as kind of strategically oriented. (ibid.)<span id="page_143" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">143</span></span></span>
</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<p>In the above quote, predictive policing was defined as requiring a greater capacity to make precise forecasts than what POL-INTEL was capable of. What is relevant for our analysis is that the Danish police and Palantir anticipate that there is a true predictive policing out there which is distinct from other forms of police attempts at forecasting the future. Notably, the idea that there is a true predictive policing is the same position which the Danish police originally held in 2016 when they presented POL-INTEL as a predictive platform. The difference in this context is that new, albeit vague, boundaries have been erected that disqualified POL-INTEL from the new definition.</p>
</div>
<div class="section" id="sec8_8">
<h3 id="d76136626e13796">All Policing is Predictive, Right?</h3>
<div class="p">After investigating the previous two examples of how predictive policing can be defined, we will now turn to a third. This definition is the most expansive as it argues—in the words of one Palantir official who rhetorically posed the question—that “all policing is predictive, right?” (Interview, Palantir Privacy &amp; Civil Liberties Engineer #2, January 2022). This position is further elaborated on by another Palantirian in response to a question of what differentiated predictive policing from ILP:<blockquote>
<p>I think it is media relations; it is the understanding that for whatever set of reasons there is this emotional reaction to this term “predictive policing.” Again, I think that leaving aside some very extremist viewpoints, nobody thinks that the police should not be acting predictively. It is a crazy idea that the police should not be acting predictively. So, then it is about which specific capabilities we think they should or shouldn’t have, and where are all the different complex lines that we want to draw are. And I don’t think that there is any difference here. We talk about ILP; it's the exact same concept. We should make sure that all the information police officers have at their disposal can be brought to bear, legally, against whatever problem the police are working on. (Interview, Palantir Scandinavia Manager, September 2021)</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<p>For the Palantir official, prediction is thus inherent in all police work. There are no boundaries or demarcations between different forms of policing in this position. This is a definition that echoes scholars such as Aradau and Blanke (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000003" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000003">2017</a>) who emphasize that pre-emption has been central for policing and governance since the inception of the modern state.</p>
<p>So far, we have shown how boundaries and demarcations around the concept of predictive policing have been erected. However, when our fieldwork took us from public discourses to police practice, these boundaries became increasingly fuzzy. For instance, the police handbook for intelligence workers, the <i>Doctrine for Intelligence and Analysis</i> (<a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000075" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000075">Rigspolitiet, 2018</a>) details an epistemic hierarchy—see below—of how police knowledge is produced, where the lowest form of knowledge is “overview”, moving up to “insight”, to <span id="page_144" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">144</span></span></span>“understanding”, and lastly to “prediction.” To be able to predict is thus posed in the Doctrine as the highest form of knowledge.</p>
<p>Similarly, when police officers detailed their everyday work, predictive techniques constantly appeared in their descriptions. This relates to intelligence officers utilizing past data to attempt to predict future crimes (Interview, Police Intelligence Analyst in District, September 2022) or how POL-INTEL's crime mapping features present crimes in an interface built to make it easier for officers to decide where to patrol (Interview, Palantir Scandinavia Manager, September 2021). When we increased the analytical focus on <i>police practice</i>, the demarcations and borders that the police and Palantir set up between ILP and predictive policing became increasingly unstable. We argue that this instability also relates to the many synonyms for predictive policing which have been developed or embraced by academics, police forces, think tanks, and government agencies. This, in turn, means that scholarly work attempting to demarcate predictive policing from other forms of police practices, whether these are called forecasts, anticipatory systems, or ILP, is liable to potential challenge.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="section" id="sec8_9">
<h2 id="d76136626e13833">Conclusions</h2>
<p>This inflation of definitions and conceptual revisionism about what predictive policing is and is not has caused considerable confusion about the affordances of POL-INTEL. One could argue that the story of this digital policing platform has followed the traditional trajectory of the construction of a black box. Following <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000053" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000053">Latour (1999)</a>, we see POL-INTEL through a process in which “scientific and technical work is made invisible by its own success. When a machine runs efficiently, when a matter of fact is settled, one needs to focus only on its inputs and outputs and not on its internal complexity. Thus, paradoxically, the more science and technology succeed, the more opaque and obscure they become” (p. 304). What we see in the case of POL-INTEL is not merely the black-boxing of a technical system, the obscuring of the procurement and implementation process, but also a critical battle around the ownership and demarcation of definitions that progressively fade away.</p>
<p>Who ascribes meaning and acquires the interpretation prerogative about what POL-INTEL is and does is directly linked to their position in the social and political hierarchy of credibility. These positions both influenced and were influenced by the imaginaries reproduced in media, which more often than not were transplanted from debates in the United States. The notion of predictive policing thus becomes a boundary object, a flexible concept that acquires positive and/or negative attributes. The latter also enabled a loose (re-)definitional flexibility. By that, it is meant that once predictive policing acquired negative connotations, Danish police and state officials re-defined the concept by <span id="page_145" class="milestone-start milestone-type-page pageBreak"><span class="popup"><span class="label">p. </span><span class="page">145</span></span></span>limiting its scope and denying its use. The shift from predictive policing as a concept to a multitude of new policing strategies is an international trend. As in the Danish example, predictive policing has become an <i>abstract concept</i> (cf. <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000088" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000088">Terpstra et al., 2019</a>). The case of POL-INTEL and predictive policing is indicative of the complex mechanisms—rhetorical, semantical, political, economic, technoscientific—at play that intensively work to establish “the great refrigerated storehouses of data” (The Invisible Committee, <a id="ref_EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000089" href="#EEP_06_CROL_C007_CIT000089">2015</a>, p. 50) as the cornerstone of current government, revising without hesitation their own concepts if needed.</p>
</div>

<div class="footnoteGroup">
<div id="ch7-fn1" class="footnote">
<span class="label"><a href="#ref_ch7-fn1">1</a></span>
<p>“Boundary work describes the process through which definitions of social phenomena come to be accepted or rejected. The central idea behind boundary work is that much of what we think of as solid or settled emerges through sustained effort to achieve this solidity and define difference” (Carlson &amp; Lewis, 2019, p. 123).</p>
</div>
</div>
